.th ENVSAVE III 3/3/76
.sh NAME
envsave, envrest \*- execute non-local goto
.sh SYNOPSIS
.ft B
envsave(&envir);
.br
int envir;
.br
	.
.br
	.
.br
	.
.br
envrest(&envir);
.ft R
.sh DESCRIPTION
Envsave saves an "environment pointer" in its argument,
.bd envir.
A subsequent
.bd envrest
with the same argument restores the environment
that existed immediately AFTER the
.bd envsave
call.
.bd Envsave
returns a
value of 1; while a
.bd envrest
appears to return from the corresponding
.bd envsave
except that the returned value is 0.
.s3
The routine that called
.bd envsave
must still be active (i.e., must
not have returned) when
.bd envrest
is called.
.s3
.bd Envsave
must be called with exactly one argument.
.s3
.bd Envsave
and
.bd envrest
are very similar in effect to
.bd setexit
(III) and
.bd reset
(III), in that they provide a means of resuming execution
in a previously established environment and aborting a chain of
procedure calls.
The major differences are:
.s3
.lp +6 5
(1)	\fIEnvsave\fR and
\fIenvrest\fR permit saving more than one environment.
.s3
.lp +6 5
(2)	The returned values of
.bd envsave
and
.bd envrest
are different, so the
return from
.bd envsave
that saves the environment can be
distinguished from subsequent
\fIenvrest\fRs.
.s3
.lp +6 5
(3)	There may be more than one
.bd envrest
call for one
.bd envsave,
since
the
.bd envrest
restores the environment that existed AFTER,
rather than before, the
.bd envsave
call.
.s3
Example:
.s3
	int saveloc;
.br
	if (envsave(&saveloc))
.br
	{/* code to be executed on return from envsave */
.br
		...
.br
	}
.br
	else
.br
	{/* code to be executed on return from envrest */
.br
		...
.br
	}
.i0
.dt
